00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.429 align:start position:0%
 
and i'm the youth development director  for the ymca of southern nevada  okay and i saw online that the  centennial hills ymca is doing a team  program um the next one is coming up  this saturday tell me a little bit about  that program and uh what the purpose of  it is  yeah absolutely so in 2017 the  centennial hills neighborhood  experienced a rise in a teen crime and  teen mischief and so  organizations were coming to the y as  one of the leading providers of youth  development programming  in the nation to ask what services we  had  in response to this local need at the  time we had  our our teen summer day camp program  and we had a few volunteer opportunities  but we realized that in this moment we  needed to expand and that's exactly what  we did  we first created a teen internship  program within the summer day camp  program  and so summer campers within the teen  group  could interview for apply for and become  an intern and shadow under some of our  executives and directors  and at the end of their six-week journey  they were to pitch a program that the y  would consider launching and over the  course of the next couple of years the  teams pitched several programs that we  have  started like robotics and coding  programs and all sorts of really fun  things  but of course one of the things that  they really wanted to do was  more teen programs during the school  year and so we launched  over the last couple of years three new  teen programs and then we just launched  another one  late in the in the fall of 2020 so the  three  main school year team programs now are  team talks  teen connection and team night they  occur on the first and third saturday of  every month  uh they are there's a nominal fee for  our members and there's financial  assistance for those  who need additional support they're back  to back so teens usually get here  around one and then they get picked up  around nine which is what they  and their families found ideal the teen  talks program is really  uh heavily geared towards youth mental  health which is especially important  right now in pandemic times  with rising rates of youth depression  and unfortunately youth  suicides so teen talks partners which  with bridge counseling associates one of  the  premier nonprofit mental health  organizations here in southern nevada  they send out staff to lead structured  but open discussions on topics that the  teens  want to talk about so it's a safe space  for them to have productive  conversations on topics like  peer pressure and substance abuse and  yes even  depression and suicide then they move  into  the teen connection program right after  and team connection is all about  service learning and life skill  development so the teens engage  in a wide range of volunteerism projects  within the community  be that with the nevada  animal shelters with the  cancer organizations cancer groups  be that a whole bunch of different  projects after they combine  those service learning projects with  life skill development opportunities so  they get to learn from  those experiences out in the community  with animal shelters with cancer  organizations and then they mold them  into  lessons that they taught themselves to  inform future projects  and then they bring in outside parties  to teach them  more things so for example the teens a  couple years ago  said that they had they knew somebody  that was hearing impaired  up here and so they wanted to learn sign  language so we brought in somebody  and they spent one of their life skill  development days learning about sign  language  uh later on we have this session we have  somebody coming out  to teach them about money management and  financial literacy  and so they uh they get to do all these  really cool life skills development  projects  um throughout the session then they go  into team night which is all social  activities so we do things like  murder mystery nights uh disco nights  all sorts of these really fun  uh themed experiences where they get to  just kind of let loose  uh and be kids have fun yeah  wow this sounds like such a  comprehensive  program it really touches on so many  factors of a teenager's life and what  they're concerned about and what they  think is important  um i really like that you guys bring in  the teenagers to talk about these  programs what they want  how important is it to collaborate with  the youth in our area  absolutely it's especially important  youth voice and youth choice are  especially important especially  in these adolescent ages teens have  a lot of complacent options today right  they can stay home and they can play  video games  and many of them lately have been forced  to do that  they can just stick to their  neighborhood or  when they're left unsupervised because  parents have to work and can't afford  child care they're left to their own  volition and that's what caused  the rise in teen crime and mischief that  i talked about which launched these teen  programs  but to convince them to let go of these  other  more complacent opportunities and and  come to do something that's productive  something that develops  them into tomorrow's community leaders  requires that they are at the helm of  the decision-making process from  building the framework  all the way through execution and so  every component  of all of our team programs so i've  mentioned  three there are several others that we  have  the teens are at the helm from the very  beginning all the way  through they pick the topics for teen  talks they pick their service learning  projects they pick their  life skill development opportunities  they pick their themes for teen night  we have a program called the dreamer lab  in partnership with charlie rocket and  cox communications  at our heinrich facility in our durango  hills facility  those again are spaces where the teens  get  to decide how to create within them  so they come to these very very unique  spaces  and there are really qualified staff  that will guide them of course  but they're at the home of what they end  up wanting to  grow into that's so great and  for centennial hills for in terms of  that you know crime that was going on  with  the teenagers there why is that is it  because there wasn't  many options for them to you know it's a  growing community they're adding more  things to do but it was it because there  wasn't as much for teenagers to do out  there did you guys ever discuss  that yeah absolutely it's it's simply  because  of that that complacency factor there's  a a common  misconception that once my child is 12  13  they can stay home alone but  unfortunately their brain isn't fully  developed they're still relying on  instinctual behaviors right they're not  thinking through  their decisions from from a to z and so  it's very easy to get into  mischief and then from mischief it's  very easy to get into criminal behavior  and that's exactly what was happening  the  the wave was dubbed the caught on camera  crimes by teen's wave  so it was teams going to houses and  stealing amazon packages  throwing rocks at windows and and  it just evolved from that so again it's  it's this something that the y is  is constantly working on is promoting  the fact that the why  is a hub for youth and adults of all  ages  all abilities and especially all income  levels a common  barrier to accessing programs is that  financial barrier but  as a non-profit we eliminate that  barrier we will work with whatever  income you have to make sure we develop  either you or your child into the person  that reaches their full potential wow  and  for people that want to take part in  this for teenagers um  do they have to belong to the centennial  hills ymca how does that work  the cool thing about the y is uh we've  got four branches here in southern  nevada once you join one you're a member  of all of them  so there are lots of different programs  at all four of our centers  uh you join at one and you get access  to all of them a substantial amount of  them are free or nominal fee like the  teen programs  and then there are some that come with a  cost offset  so things like summer camp for example  comes with an additional cost  however of course what's extremely  important for uh for the y is that no  one will ever be turned away due to the  inability to pay  so you join based on your financial  circumstances and then you sign up for  the programs  uh based on your financial circumstances  we will take care of the rest  um that's really great especially in you  know what's going on right now with so  many people losing  their work and things like that um is  there anything else that you want to add  about the program anything i didn't ask  about that's important for parents and  their teenagers to know about the  about what's going on with you guys no i  think i think  you've done a great job touching base on  on some of these really important  programs  i'll say that  teens initially want to stay home  and stay within their comfort zone but  something that we learn even as adults  as you can't grow within your comfort  zone  so i would encourage parents to take a  look at the why  there's a lot of different programs  within teams you've got six or seven  different offerings  uh by themselves find the one or two  that fits  what you are hoping to get out of the  experience and then allow the y to  support you  uh from the financial assistance aspect  if if you need that assistance  and then if you're in the community and  you'd like to uh participate in these  programs we're always looking  for donors and volunteers to support and  grow this work

